---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "Weslo Cadence G 5.9 Review (2026): Discontinued, What Now";
const description =
  "For years the best-selling budget treadmill in America, and now discontinued. What the Cadence G 5.9 actually was, what it is worth used, and what to buy instead.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "weslo-cadence-g-5-9",
    name: "Weslo Cadence G 5.9",
    brand: "Weslo",
    category: "Discontinued Budget Treadmill",
    badge: "Discontinued",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Weslo%20Cadence%20G%205.9+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$150-$300 used",
    priceBounds: {"low":150,"high":300},
    specs: "50 x 16 in. deck · 2.25 HP · two manual incline positions · 0-10 mph · 275 lb capacity",
    pros: [
      "Light enough at roughly 118 lb for one person to move and fold without help",
      "Genuinely inexpensive, which is the entire reason it sold in the numbers it did",
      "Simple console with no account, no platform and nothing to lapse",
      "Widely sold, so used examples and spare parts are relatively easy to find",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Discontinued — no new stock, and long-term parts availability is a real question",
      "16-inch belt width is narrow enough to be noticeable while walking",
      "Incline is two manual positions you set by hand, not a motorised range",
      "118 lb machine weight means perceptible movement under anything faster than a walk",
    ],
    bottomLine: "A fair machine for its price when new, and now only worth buying used, cheaply, after you have run it under power. Budget $150-$300 and no more.",
    description: "The Cadence G 5.9 paired a 2.25 HP motor with a 16 by 50-inch belt, two manually set incline positions, a 10 mph ceiling and a published 275 lb capacity, in a folding frame weighing around 118 lb with six preset programmes. Read as a set, those figures describe exactly what it was: the cheapest machine a major manufacturer was willing to attach its name to, built for walking in a spare room by one person a few times a week. It did that job adequately for a very long time and sold in numbers almost nothing else in the category has matched. It has since been discontinued, which changes the question from \"should I buy one\" to \"what is one worth, and for how much longer will parts exist\".",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Reviews", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/reviews/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Weslo Cadence G 5.9", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/weslo-cadence-g-5-9-treadmill/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Weslo Cadence G 5.9 still available?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Weslo's treadmill line has been retired and this model is discontinued, so anything you find will be used or old stock. That does not make a working example a bad buy at the right price, but it does mean no warranty, no manufacturer support, and a parts supply with a finite horizon — which is the first thing to ask about before paying.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much is a used Weslo Cadence G 5.9 worth?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Realistically $150-$300 for a machine you have run under power and walked on, and less for anything you have not tested. Above that range, a used example of a current mid-range model or a cleared new budget machine is the better purchase, because both come with parts support and one of them comes with a warranty.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you run on a Weslo Cadence G 5.9?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not comfortably, and not regularly. The 16 by 50-inch belt is small in both dimensions, the frame weighs around 118 lb which is too light to stay planted at pace, and the incline is two manual positions rather than a motorised range. It was built for walking by one user, and that is the use it does well.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What replaced the Weslo Cadence G 5.9?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Nothing directly, which is why it is missed. The closest modern equivalents are walking pads and under-desk treadmills, which cover what most owners actually used it for at a similar price with less compromise. If you want a full-size folding machine with motorised incline, the real step up is the $650-$900 band.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the weight capacity of the Weslo Cadence G 5.9?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The published figure is 275 lb. Read it alongside the machine's own weight of roughly 118 lb, because frame mass is what resists the movement your footfall puts into the deck. A light frame feels light underfoot at anything above walking pace regardless of its rated capacity, and that is the honest limit here.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Weslo Cadence G 5.9 the same as the G 5.9i?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No, and this is a common source of confusion in listings. Weslo sold several closely named variants with different consoles and occasionally different specifications. Figures quoted for one are not reliable for another, so check the model number on the sticker on the frame rather than trusting a listing title or an old review.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-09-15",
      dateModified: "2026-09-15",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 1,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/weslo-cadence-g-5-9-treadmill/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/weslo-cadence-g-5-9-treadmill/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "reviews");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-09-15",
    modifiedTime: "2026-09-15",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "weslo cadence g 5.9",
      "weslo treadmill",
      "weslo cadence g 5.9 treadmill",
      "budget treadmill",
      "used treadmill",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Discontinued: Weslo Cadence G 5.9", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/reviews/">Reviews</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Model Review</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Weslo Cadence G 5.9 Treadmill Review</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        It sold in enormous numbers by being the cheapest folding treadmill a major brand would put its name on. It has been discontinued — so this review is about what it was, what it is worth secondhand, and what replaces it.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated September 15, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#weslo-cadence-g-5-9" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          The Cadence G 5.9 was, for the better part of a decade, the answer to "what is the
          cheapest treadmill that is not junk". It accumulated thousands of reviews, sold through
          every big-box retailer in the country, and has now been discontinued. If you are
          searching for it, you are almost certainly looking at a used listing — so that is what
          this page is written for.
        </p>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the numbers below and the model name.</strong> These
          are the published figures for the Cadence G 5.9, cross-checked in September 2026 against
          retailer listings and independent review coverage. They are not our own measurements.
          Weslo sold several closely named variants — the G 5.9i among them — with different
          consoles and occasionally different specifications, so figures quoted against one are
          not reliable for another. Verify against the sticker on the frame of the actual machine
          you are looking at.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#why-it-sold">Why It Sold in Those Numbers</a></li>
            <li><a href="#the-specs-honestly">The Specifications, Read Honestly</a></li>
            <li><a href="#discontinued">It Is Discontinued. What That Actually Means for You</a></li>
            <li><a href="#buying-used">If You Are Looking at a Used One</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-to-buy-instead">What to Buy Instead</a></li>
            <li><a href="#keeping-one-alive">If You Already Own One</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="why-it-sold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Why It Sold in Those Numbers</h2>
<p>
            The Cadence G 5.9 was never the best treadmill at anything. It was the cheapest one
            from a recognisable manufacturer, available everywhere, light enough to handle alone,
            and adequate for walking — and that combination turns out to be what an enormous number
            of buyers actually want.
          </p>
          <p>
            It also arrived with no ongoing commitment. There was no console platform, no
            membership, nothing to lapse and nothing to log into. You plugged it in and it worked.
            In a category that spent the following decade moving toward subscription-led hardware,
            that plainness aged better than anyone expected — see <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our subscription-free guide</a> for why that property is worth more than it looks.
          </p>
          <p>
            The honest counterpoint is that its reputation was built on price rather than
            engineering. A 16 by 50-inch belt is small, a 2.25 HP rating is modest and quoted
            without the continuous-duty qualifier that would make it comparable, and 118 lb of
            machine is not enough mass to stay planted under anything more than a walk. None of
            that made it a bad purchase at its price. All of it makes it a poor comparison against
            machines costing two or three times as much, which is a comparison the search results
            will constantly invite you to make.
          </p>
          <p>
            Read as a walking machine for one light-to-average user, in a spare room, a few times a
            week, it did its job. Read as anything else, it never did.
          </p>
        </div>
      <!-- Product blocks -->
      <div class="not-prose">
        {
          PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <section id={product.id} class="ed-product">
              <div class="ed-p-head">
                <div class="ed-p-num">{index + 1}</div>
                <div>
                  <div class="ed-p-award">{product.badge}</div>
                  <h3 class="ed-p-name">{product.name}</h3>
                  <p class="ed-p-cat">{product.category}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class={`ed-p-media${product.image ? " has-img" : ""}`}>
                {product.image && ( <a href={product.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="ed-p-img">
                    <img src={product.image.url} alt={product.name} width={product.image.width} height={product.image.height} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                  </a> )}
                <div class="ed-p-buy">
                  <a href={product.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy">
                    <span>{product.amazonLabel.replace(/ on Amazon$/, "")}</span>
                    <span>Amazon &rarr;</span>
                  </a>
                  <span class="ed-buy-note">
                    {product.priceRange && <><strong>Typical price:</strong> {product.priceRange}. </>}
                    Prices and availability change often; Amazon shows today's price.
                  </span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="ed-proscons">
                <div class="pros">
                  <h4>Pros</h4>
                  <ul>{product.pros.map((pro) => <li>{pro}</li>)}</ul>
                </div>
                <div class="cons">
                  <h4>Cons</h4>
                  <ul>{product.cons.map((con) => <li>{con}</li>)}</ul>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="ed-p-copy">
                <p><span class="ed-lbl">Why we picked it:</span> <Fragment set:html={product.description} /></p>
                <p><span class="ed-lbl">Bottom line:</span> {product.bottomLine}</p>
              </div>
              {product.specs && (
                <>
                  <div class="ed-glance-h">Features at a glance</div>
                  <div class="ed-glance">
                    {product.priceRange && <div><span class="g-k">Price</span>{product.priceRange}</div>}
                    {product.specs.split(/\s+·\s+/).filter(Boolean).map((s) => <div>{s}</div>)}
                  </div>
                </>
              )}
              <p class="ed-p-links">
                <a href={product.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener">See the {product.name} on Amazon &rarr;</a> {product.reviewUrl && <a href={product.reviewUrl} class="ed-p-more">Read our full {product.name} review &rarr;</a>}
              </p>
            </section>
          ))
        }
      </div>

        <div id="the-specs-honestly" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Specifications, Read Honestly</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The deck is the constraint, and it is smaller than it sounds.</strong> Fifty
            inches by sixteen is short and narrow even by budget standards — most machines in the
            tier above run 55 by 20. The width is the part people notice first: sixteen inches
            gives very little margin for the natural wander in foot placement that develops over a
            long walk, and the rails are closer than you expect. Our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a> covers what deck dimensions mean for different strides.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The motor figure is not comparable to a CHP rating.</strong> A 2.25 HP number
            quoted without "continuous duty" cannot be compared against the 2.5 CHP machines in the
            tier above — the two describe different things, and the peak-versus-continuous
            distinction is exactly what our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a> exists to explain. Treat it as adequate for walking and unproven for anything sustained.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The incline is manual.</strong> Two fixed positions you set by hand before you
            start, not a motorised range you adjust mid-session. That rules out incline intervals
            and the kind of structured walking protocol that makes a treadmill genuinely effective
            — <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the 12-3-30 protocol</a>,
            for instance, is not possible on this machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The capacity and the machine weight tell different stories.</strong> A 275 lb
            published capacity on a 118 lb frame is an unusual ratio. Machine mass is what resists
            the movement your footfall puts into the deck, and at 118 lb there is not much of it,
            which is why the machine feels light underfoot at anything above a walk regardless of
            what the capacity figure says. Our rule of body weight plus 25% still applies, but read
            the frame weight alongside it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="discontinued" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>It Is Discontinued. What That Actually Means for You</h2>
<p>
            Weslo's treadmill line has been retired, and the Cadence G 5.9 with it. That does not
            make an existing machine stop working, and it does not make a used one a bad buy. It
            changes three specific things, and they are worth being clear about.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Parts availability becomes a clock.</strong> Belts, motor controllers and
            console boards for discontinued machines remain available for a while through
            third-party suppliers and then quietly stop. For a machine that sold in these numbers
            the tail will be long — but it is a tail, and a controller board failure five years
            from now may simply end the machine. That is the single most important question to ask
            before paying anything for one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Warranty is gone.</strong> Any used example is sold as-is, with no recourse and
            no service path. Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used buying checklist</a> is the fifteen checks to run before money changes hands, and the <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new versus used comparison</a> covers what you are actually trading away.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The price should reflect both.</strong> A discontinued entry-level machine with
            no warranty and a finite parts horizon is worth meaningfully less than a used example of
            a current model. Somewhere in the $150-$300 range is realistic for a working, tested
            machine; above that, a current-model used treadmill or a cleared new one is the better
            purchase — see <a href="/treadmills-for-sale/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to buy and what to pay</a> for how those markets compare.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>If You Are Looking at a Used One</h2>
<p>
            Run it under power before money changes hands. This is not negotiable on any used
            treadmill and it matters more here, because the most expensive failure on a machine
            like this — the motor controller — presents to an untrained buyer as something trivial.
            "It just needs a belt" is the most costly sentence in the used market.
          </p>
          <p>
            Walk on it at 2 mph and then at 4. Listen for a squeal that changes pitch with speed,
            feel for hesitation as your foot lands, and watch whether the belt drifts toward one
            rail. Then set both incline positions by hand and confirm the frame locks solidly in
            each. Those checks catch most of what is expensive: <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt slipping</a>, <a href="/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">bearing and roller noise</a>,
            and a motor labouring under load.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ask how it was stored and whether the deck was ever lubricated. The honest answer from
            most owners is no, which is not a reason to walk away but is a reason to budget for a
            lubrication and a <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>belt tension</a> check in the first week — <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the maintenance checklist</a> is the ten minutes that decides whether you got a bargain.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, check you can move it. At around 118 lb this is one of the few treadmills a
            single capable person can genuinely handle, which is a real advantage of this
            particular machine and the reason so many of them change hands. Confirm the folding
            lock engages positively before you carry it anywhere, and take the stairs with a second
            person even though the weight suggests you do not need one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy-instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Buy Instead</h2>
<p>
            If what attracted you to the Cadence G 5.9 was the price, be honest about what that
            budget buys today. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$500 guide</a> covers the tier directly, and its conclusion is worth repeating: at this budget a
            walking pad is frequently a better product than a compromised full-size treadmill,
            because it does one job properly instead of three jobs badly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Under-desk treadmills and walking pads</a> are the direct modern replacement for what this machine was mostly used for — walking,
            indoors, without much space. They are lighter, quieter, store more easily and cost
            about the same, and they do not pretend to be running machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want a full-size folding treadmill with a proper motorised incline, the next
            genuine step up is the $650-$900 band, and it is a large step in quality rather than a
            small one. The <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon T101</a> is the subscription-free pick there, the <a href="/proform-505-cst-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm 505 CST</a> carries the highest capacity in the tier, and the <a href="/nordictrack-t-6-5-s-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack T 6.5 S</a> is the cheapest way into a content platform. All three have motorised incline, longer
            and wider decks, and current parts support.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if the budget is genuinely fixed and low, buying a used example of a current
            mid-range machine beats buying a new budget one almost every time — roughly twice the
            hardware per dollar, provided you inspect properly. That is the argument our <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new versus used guide</a> makes in full.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="keeping-one-alive" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>If You Already Own One</h2>
<p>
            Keep it running, because the economics of repairing an entry-level machine turn against
            you quickly and the economics of maintaining one never do. Ten minutes a month is the
            difference between this machine lasting another five years and failing next winter.
          </p>
          <p>
            Lubricate the deck on the interval the manual specifies. A dry deck increases motor
            load and wears the belt from the underside, and on a machine with a small motor that
            matters sooner than it does on a large one. <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">The walkthrough</a> covers the method, <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how often</a> covers the interval, and <a href="/can-you-use-wd40-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the WD-40 question</a> covers the mistake that ends more budget decks than wear does.
          </p>
          <p>
            Keep the belt correctly tensioned and tracking centrally, diagnosing those as the two
            separate faults they are — <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">slipping versus tracking</a> walks through the order. <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Vacuum the motor</a> compartment periodically, because a light
            machine has a small compartment and dust accumulates faster than you would think.
          </p>
          <p>
            When something does fail, price the repair against a replacement honestly. With no
            warranty, no manufacturer support and a finite parts supply, the point at which a
            repair stops being worth paying for arrives earlier than it would on a current machine
            — <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our lifespan guide</a> covers that arithmetic, and <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the troubleshooting guide</a> covers the faults worth attempting first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes With a Cadence G 5.9</h2>
          <p>This machine is now bought almost entirely secondhand, and the errors that matter are the ones specific to buying a discontinued product.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Paying current-model money for a discontinued machine</h3>
              <p>No warranty, no manufacturer support and a parts supply on a clock should all be reflected in the price. A working, tested example is worth $150-$300; anything materially above that is better spent on a used current-model machine or a cleared new one, both of which come with a service path this does not have.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying it without running it under power</h3>
              <p>The motor controller is the expensive failure on machines like this and it presents to an untrained buyer as something trivial. Five minutes at 2 mph and 4 mph, listening for pitch changes and watching for belt drift, is what separates a bargain from a very awkward disposal problem. A seller who cannot demonstrate the machine is telling you something.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Comparing its 2.25 HP against 2.5 CHP machines</h3>
              <p>A horsepower figure quoted without the continuous-duty qualifier is not comparable to a CHP rating — they describe different things, and peak figures flatter the machine quoting them. This is the single most common way budget treadmills are made to look competitive against the tier above, and it is worth recognising on any listing, not just this one.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Expecting incline work from two manual positions</h3>
              <p>The incline here is set by hand before you start, not adjusted mid-session, which rules out incline intervals and structured walking protocols entirely. If incline walking is the reason you want a treadmill — and for most people it should be — this machine cannot do it, and that is a specification difference rather than a matter of degree.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            The Cadence G 5.9 earned its sales: it was the cheapest folding treadmill a major brand
            would stand behind, and it walked people around their spare rooms for years. It is <strong class="text-white">discontinued</strong>, so the only question left is what a
            used one is worth — and the answer is $150-$300, after you have run it.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you wanted it for the price, look at <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">walking pads</a> instead, which do that job better today. If you wanted a full-size folding machine, the <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Horizon T101</a> is the honest step up, with a real motorised incline and parts you can still buy.
          </p>
        </div>

