        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is the Weslo Cadence G 5.9 still available?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Weslo's treadmill line has been retired and this model is discontinued, so anything you find will be used or old stock. That does not make a working example a bad buy at the right price, but it does mean no warranty, no manufacturer support, and a parts supply with a finite horizon — which is the first thing to ask about before paying.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much is a used Weslo Cadence G 5.9 worth?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Realistically $150-$300 for a machine you have run under power and walked on, and less for anything you have not tested. Above that range, a used example of a current mid-range model or a cleared new budget machine is the better purchase, because both come with parts support and one of them comes with a warranty.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you run on a Weslo Cadence G 5.9?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not comfortably, and not regularly. The 16 by 50-inch belt is small in both dimensions, the frame weighs around 118 lb which is too light to stay planted at pace, and the incline is two manual positions rather than a motorised range. It was built for walking by one user, and that is the use it does well.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What replaced the Weslo Cadence G 5.9?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Nothing directly, which is why it is missed. The closest modern equivalents are walking pads and under-desk treadmills, which cover what most owners actually used it for at a similar price with less compromise. If you want a full-size folding machine with motorised incline, the real step up is the $650-$900 band.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the weight capacity of the Weslo Cadence G 5.9?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The published figure is 275 lb. Read it alongside the machine's own weight of roughly 118 lb, because frame mass is what resists the movement your footfall puts into the deck. A light frame feels light underfoot at anything above walking pace regardless of its rated capacity, and that is the honest limit here.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Weslo Cadence G 5.9 the same as the G 5.9i?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No, and this is a common source of confusion in listings. Weslo sold several closely named variants with different consoles and occasionally different specifications. Figures quoted for one are not reliable for another, so check the model number on the sticker on the frame rather than trusting a listing title or an old review.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

